#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93415651-fbcb-56d3-ad93-2b7f34e7bf4c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Negated Sentence in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "399b4c40-eb9f-58b3-95bf-8b5a328ab828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399160} {:answer/id #uuid "bb42fe88-30ff-5d4a-9ddc-7267e2860a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∨Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399161} {:answer/id #uuid "8249b093-9fe6-5147-b1d5-815d065e1a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399162} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2a53ee-7270-5777-87a4-50b35f9c3e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∧Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399163} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e68e25-6dfe-5220-94ef-0f37adf8b6aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb42fe88-30ff-5d4a-9ddc-7267e2860a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∨Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399161} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a89e174-5e46-555a-a1ed-145e95644310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf817965-a661-536e-b9bc-f7b88632bc85" :question/math-academy-id "q-81372" :question/problem "$1$ is **not** prime or composite number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $P:1\\text{ is prime}$\n\n- $Q:1\\text{ is composite}$" :question/worked-solution "The given sentence is the negation of the following sentence: \n\n$1$ is prime or a composite number, \n\nwhich can be expressed symbolically as $P∨Q.$\n\nThe negation is represented by the symbol \"$¬$\". \n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as $¬(P∨Q).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93415651-fbcb-56d3-ad93-2b7f34e7bf4c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Negated Sentence in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa470d03-c579-5191-9d6e-817237797af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨¬Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399167} {:answer/id #uuid "be756830-49dc-5e43-9651-def9f8fc55f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∨Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399168} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0e58c2-034a-5988-b77b-6f3ef046dde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∧Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399169} {:answer/id #uuid "523005d0-2bae-5503-80be-1e748c72d8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399170} {:answer/id #uuid "9348944a-e977-519b-8cc8-d0c7b6b75730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∨¬Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be756830-49dc-5e43-9651-def9f8fc55f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬(P∨Q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399168} :answer-field/id #uuid "578e3994-2713-5089-b174-35403fc9151c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0be84e8-e6bc-50b7-bab9-62f8c1a915a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81402" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** an integer or rational number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $P:\\sqrt{2}\\text{ is an integer number}$\n\n- $Q:\\sqrt{2}\\text{ is a rational number}$" :question/worked-solution "The given sentence is the negation of the following sentence: \n\n$\\sqrt{2}$ is an integer or rational number, \n\nwhich can be expressed symbolically as $P∨Q.$\n\nThe negation is represented by the symbol \"$¬$\". \n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as $¬(P∨Q).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6965c4f-a2b7-5188-a3a3-a57d5dc63484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399174} {:answer/id #uuid "25e57da5-1b38-5f72-b567-d5c1afc8a6ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0<t<2$ and $t>4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399175} {:answer/id #uuid "8f086f32-f9b0-5a9f-8ea4-43774d5f1175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399176} {:answer/id #uuid "7efe2cc1-7fd1-5257-b4ae-77bc8744aca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<t<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399177} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab0b651-1995-5b9e-b502-fcc5bb486710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0<t<1$ and $t>3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7efe2cc1-7fd1-5257-b4ae-77bc8744aca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<t<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3dd0609-1503-57fb-9ef8-371d237baa26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "355a01e3-70fb-59a2-890d-575b2107fb75" :question/math-academy-id "q-81772" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-8t+3,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+3)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(1)^{3}-4(1)^{2}+3(1)+C \\\\ 0 & =1-4+3+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t=t(t-3)(t-1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f1dc2cc8c718f825c629bd92beb116326a36deb1dfbbe71e028cf1cf41445eb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ba25c0c-9ba9-586a-b66f-7f07076d2827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Growth Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad5e1f00-cc1f-5d4f-87c2-02b6b36f47ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399181} {:answer/id #uuid "cec7db82-de58-50eb-a3aa-1156e21fbeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,332" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399182} {:answer/id #uuid "34503797-dc86-5eff-9dba-4c2c0e4b5865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,377" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399183} {:answer/id #uuid "9c43e127-b476-5072-8782-64defe890eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,248" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399184} {:answer/id #uuid "96af2a00-4233-59fc-a447-2f18d08406ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,755" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cec7db82-de58-50eb-a3aa-1156e21fbeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,332" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399182} :answer-field/id #uuid "714a5ee8-c0ff-5aa3-a5d9-82c81856f170" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c60fbb60-b78a-5c24-90ef-165c91d4dd65" :question/math-academy-id "q-83144" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a video channel after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nIf the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $12$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=15\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=12,$ we substitute $t=12$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(12) & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.6} \\\\  & ≈27\\,331.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $27\\,332$ after $12$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d0fe1a-19ca-5f80-a487-821d0d2f05da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399188} {:answer/id #uuid "6e287a52-db21-559f-9d89-5103b7019f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399189} {:answer/id #uuid "f5969ae4-0089-56bd-bf20-0cf41f4333df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399190} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0d464b-7ff4-56b8-bcb7-57b51b72d32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399191} {:answer/id #uuid "db4a7149-d824-54c0-86ee-b21c01fb5cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd0d464b-7ff4-56b8-bcb7-57b51b72d32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399191} :answer-field/id #uuid "249bd719-2909-5d27-a78e-0ce0dd0aaab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97e3e680-a9e7-597f-a877-a0daf5245a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-83477" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-1,\\,x-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x-1=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ is not in the interior of $D$,  we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the line $y=2-x,$ in the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t)-(0) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t⟨-2,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2-2t,2t) \\\\  & =(2-2t)(2t)-(2-2t)-(2t) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}+4t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-4{t}^{2}+4t-2)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0),\\,(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88495c5e-727c-5bdc-8c66-f5f7a1da7068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399195} {:answer/id #uuid "973ba97a-cf4d-57f4-b6a7-6d7f845916bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399196} {:answer/id #uuid "a9de477f-3e83-5cb8-ab8c-3547e66a97cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399197} {:answer/id #uuid "89aba4dc-e9ba-5704-a200-86c4f6f35692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399198} {:answer/id #uuid "1965bab2-64b2-5353-bc34-d0701180dfe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88495c5e-727c-5bdc-8c66-f5f7a1da7068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399195} :answer-field/id #uuid "adc9838b-0769-5bf4-b3d7-c8c2300681dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae635709-cef0-5d90-bcf8-39aaac1bc474" :question/math-academy-id "q-83480" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{9}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf764b3-b5dc-5b5a-abc6-619ed240e8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399202} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff8c962-ac33-5ade-9011-9b96b78cc434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399203} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5cfc50-7130-55a5-a100-906bbe37b827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399204} {:answer/id #uuid "946b0b50-f143-58dc-b90c-fba5b6dccff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399205} {:answer/id #uuid "5869b5d8-1b29-59f0-8e8e-a890cf28490c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "946b0b50-f143-58dc-b90c-fba5b6dccff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399205} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7423f8b-a007-5757-8b22-bf2bd8ad4565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a11ee4d1-3e88-5212-80d0-293758418f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83481" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-11{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+11{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{11}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{2} & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{8}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e7db313-b4d5-5153-bcfa-dde6e87c4ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399209} {:answer/id #uuid "97309839-825e-57b1-95c6-7824e0a74346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399210} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb2cae5-16fa-5704-83b1-6c31b690cd55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399211} {:answer/id #uuid "e12f4381-2d6d-5cb7-86dc-4e456318069c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399212} {:answer/id #uuid "4958c111-ad18-51a1-bae1-88b3f960e07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12f4381-2d6d-5cb7-86dc-4e456318069c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399212} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e571167-d51a-53d3-8b9f-8daca0904597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c8cbd85-ace7-5114-80e4-3625b6d8dbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-83505" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8(1-2t)^{3},12(1+4t)^{2}⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,-26⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(1-2t)^{3},12(1+4t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(1-2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 12(1+4t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-(1-2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,(1+4t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨-1,-26⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨-(1-2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,(1+4(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-26⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-27.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-(1-2t)^{4},\\,(1+4t)^{3}-27⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-(1-2t)^{4},\\,(1+4t)^{3}-27⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(1-2t)^{4} & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ (1+4t)^{3}-27 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918b64ff-b732-50dc-9158-806369dd83c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399216} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6e5666-ea13-5af7-b833-a4815ec380df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399217} {:answer/id #uuid "3009444f-bfd8-532d-8128-d1d01ae05164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399218} {:answer/id #uuid "3576f6c2-83e5-556b-a02f-499e53dc7e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399219} {:answer/id #uuid "36a5002c-c0b9-58ab-acb7-d95d0cb5a0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "918b64ff-b732-50dc-9158-806369dd83c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399216} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7146fa8-f4db-5ae1-a574-b50b6f3b88e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ee6c969-d319-57a0-b1a1-3435c9110a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83826" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-7$ does not take the above form. The value $-7$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{7}{1},$ and the numerator $(7)$ is not a factor of $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b3f1e3-8c79-5c21-af24-2422b0553cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399223} {:answer/id #uuid "597ee6d4-7ae6-5b66-8819-c98a309ce41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399224} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f71d5e-7c62-59ce-b3eb-ece84bf182c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399225} {:answer/id #uuid "267b3288-c2ab-531e-a4ef-c630d0c9728b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399226} {:answer/id #uuid "2edfc2e7-c27b-566a-a56c-76809ee37046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "597ee6d4-7ae6-5b66-8819-c98a309ce41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399224} :answer-field/id #uuid "453d5361-60be-58a9-a449-bd8996b7f09f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e3e5aa9-3c15-5f62-9549-6ee08ad4a6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84598" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67455965-c9d3-5ae8-8b56-2a58af95df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3ebdac7-d16e-5300-a6fd-1a829dfad4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399229} {:answer/id #uuid "7a586be8-2692-5d04-a4b6-f033c6bb6e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399230} {:answer/id #uuid "580374af-f90d-5946-93b1-8b98eeca722e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399231} {:answer/id #uuid "69567e9b-b328-5409-a814-a865891daade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399232} {:answer/id #uuid "05a1e094-9bd4-548c-aba3-efb60c44de4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ebdac7-d16e-5300-a6fd-1a829dfad4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399229} :answer-field/id #uuid "288c02c3-927f-5b09-9001-624966a70aa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d578c6cc-9bea-57a7-8928-c304b5f6c737" :question/math-academy-id "q-84829" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=12y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(3)y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=3.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae026cc-2f8b-57ac-b71d-10c48af3c603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399236} {:answer/id #uuid "2fff83ba-a026-5a48-a91c-bd58ef842ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399237} {:answer/id #uuid "6242ac02-1910-5a41-abad-40594fc76cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399238} {:answer/id #uuid "624befe8-6b9c-5657-a58f-1250e92d2f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399239} {:answer/id #uuid "b7dae3f1-f3d4-52af-ab47-2cc200f1b13b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "624befe8-6b9c-5657-a58f-1250e92d2f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399239} :answer-field/id #uuid "c119d922-a10c-5cfb-9f5d-697a1f5368d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "179e9be2-d5dc-5bf7-a08f-f77d3bd121bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-85966" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e03226558b1dbb2e93d23947b0b05f3d8413dc35207bbe9b536e68108f40955.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ while the $5\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. If the volume of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $6\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $2\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =15\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=6\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $z$ is not changing and $z=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=5xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5xy) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =5x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+5y(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =5(6)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+5(2)(-3) \\\\ 15 & =30(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-30 \\\\ 45 & =30(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=6\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be8d0a7-6de2-5a8a-bb9b-dce0d9c14190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399242} {:answer/id #uuid "e998af51-e20b-5b84-bd4a-a9de461a347f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399243} {:answer/id #uuid "43749bb5-2143-5e1c-a7a5-011564260c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399244} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9ae71a-f628-55f5-b849-79f13018ee74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399245} {:answer/id #uuid "0355d7bc-5328-5a46-8f44-4dda5270bea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e998af51-e20b-5b84-bd4a-a9de461a347f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399243} :answer-field/id #uuid "8309d1f3-a727-53df-8a35-d087b45480a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "137cbf82-f6fa-5b07-a735-e5850a186305" :question/math-academy-id "q-86975" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+6y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5x+6y \\\\  & =5\\cdot (0)+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "963f3713-419f-5a42-a8fd-36820ed2b9d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399249} {:answer/id #uuid "77d0fe15-2bfd-57f0-991d-684ad2dfd05b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399250} {:answer/id #uuid "a30c29de-8bbd-5ce5-b082-0d86e79ae9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399251} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de3ee5-4e26-5981-b4bd-fa52650fe448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399252} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0fd701-7b05-5e6d-9f51-cb1531e040d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed0fd701-7b05-5e6d-9f51-cb1531e040d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399253} :answer-field/id #uuid "00acfb45-f915-59a3-94c8-f02ca6e1865b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "896ad0b2-d79e-5667-ae9b-69d97f290fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89417" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6391f63d-2767-50fd-8d6d-399705dd193e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s-2)(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399255} {:answer/id #uuid "7c27cdcd-45ec-5582-8de2-98843f585000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399256} {:answer/id #uuid "45721693-741e-5a77-adf1-966cec978820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(s-2)(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399257} {:answer/id #uuid "8662e7b5-1e0f-55ac-97f1-c70dd6d8b3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s-6}{(s+2)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399258} {:answer/id #uuid "cecda9c9-be1d-5b87-8565-27cba4405226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{(s+2)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c27cdcd-45ec-5582-8de2-98843f585000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399256} :answer-field/id #uuid "b29f83ae-6ce7-5677-8e73-4e7762c39fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5620868a-e836-565c-a7cc-7e2d887588f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-89589" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{-{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-(s+2)}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-2,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f5a553a-2376-55cd-bc5e-82413407a146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399262} {:answer/id #uuid "0e31193a-a47b-5236-90bf-56a2004b1817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399263} {:answer/id #uuid "fd17c3ac-bd9b-5d08-b0bf-0332bb052907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399264} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f48a49-3778-5c56-a656-600e04741a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399265} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f6cb90-2038-5a8d-9cb0-03bdb5ac61c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+16{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f5a553a-2376-55cd-bc5e-82413407a146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399262} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30d8193-6f9e-52bd-a96a-3e323a570917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "873cc1aa-ff8b-506b-b81a-49f440b9d6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94267" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4a94922-1625-5d15-b68a-d68cb15f03f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399269} {:answer/id #uuid "910cdfa2-d7b4-5e59-a5b6-0e5abae3d765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399270} {:answer/id #uuid "319779d2-534b-5610-b6a9-b434baf8c080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399271} {:answer/id #uuid "46208be2-e44d-501e-b2d0-174fb33ac5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399272} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b7aa89-dd35-5e5d-85c6-68109bf39843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46208be2-e44d-501e-b2d0-174fb33ac5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399272} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d92405-6087-5254-97d5-0303eb88a32f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "722a3581-8ce0-5ec8-baf3-aa06bcfd2a36" :question/math-academy-id "q-94268" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe0ac12-3cc1-55d6-b9bf-ff24251e83b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399276} {:answer/id #uuid "c9daf291-4613-5128-b807-55231aa6b7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399277} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0438ff-3859-5e07-b762-c9106e8d2b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399278} {:answer/id #uuid "0be2561c-49b3-5f8b-ad20-1a5654de6657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399279} {:answer/id #uuid "b44c6ba9-a1db-5d4e-b228-ff157e3abe87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe0ac12-3cc1-55d6-b9bf-ff24251e83b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399276} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d55ca46-1e37-53b2-a389-dff0a71c6234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cf6d9af-b061-5993-b553-d544e56a866d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94283" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2\\sqrt{2}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=2\\sqrt{2}R\\,⟹\\,R=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e0b4012-403b-5739-8f33-3c79111129fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Elliptic Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067751b8-d9b0-5947-868e-d67de8882ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399283} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbc0f20-e5f6-5a52-a355-67a35255c6ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399284} {:answer/id #uuid "a552dd66-26cc-5d78-8f7f-b71e1309952a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399285} {:answer/id #uuid "49d58c58-ca20-5f66-a319-cbbd744cd1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,10,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399286} {:answer/id #uuid "6429d9be-dd6f-5794-a79d-50e42b99b671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dbc0f20-e5f6-5a52-a355-67a35255c6ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399284} :answer-field/id #uuid "27aca539-4253-563c-9949-e198ffdfa1f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ed2d339-1669-5c85-b00e-540acdbbec95" :question/math-academy-id "q-94609" :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $4(x-1)^{2}+10(y-4)^{2}={z}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+10(y-4)^{2} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{1/10} & =(z-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(1,4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c610096-f546-51ed-933a-3503175a90be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399290} {:answer/id #uuid "0b54db3c-8d6d-526f-a175-03ba6ce2af5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399291} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0e9dfa-1e7d-5276-a512-39501b2a2767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399292} {:answer/id #uuid "88dcd79e-fa94-516e-9b4f-1e0158b64a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399293} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb12f7f-6eed-5940-a40a-4ec06ffa1f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb12f7f-6eed-5940-a40a-4ec06ffa1f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399294} :answer-field/id #uuid "5009b981-acee-537f-a90c-d9fc4554b09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20513819-0985-5cdd-a8ca-960c927c6ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-95216" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 4$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R))=6\\cdot 4=24.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0adb681-dc77-54fc-9307-f54287fe979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:X\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399296} {:answer/id #uuid "82c1c1cb-051e-5f0a-9b5d-0cf75a3feb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Q\\to X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399297} {:answer/id #uuid "e098e76f-5a82-5b93-8abb-179c4f6ed79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:X\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399298} {:answer/id #uuid "e747946f-6334-5eba-9d21-6f6c633c5247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399299} {:answer/id #uuid "5c54d3a3-0df7-5d93-8f9f-f305d178abca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0adb681-dc77-54fc-9307-f54287fe979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:X\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399296} :answer-field/id #uuid "303a5f01-6e9e-5b66-add2-15ba03dcf538" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25726fd8-78e9-529e-acdf-5c4c5a237b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-96040" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of real numbers.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of real numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:X\\to R$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $X,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ab0437-c045-5c7f-b38c-be5cb1712a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399303} {:answer/id #uuid "907e5e7f-7e94-57d6-9bfb-14bd7f6ece40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399304} {:answer/id #uuid "0e159865-11e2-5161-bb1a-c643efc6678d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399305} {:answer/id #uuid "57e76023-479d-5f84-af88-1fb8fed76692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399306} {:answer/id #uuid "2546f306-2d7a-5ce6-8172-c0754a16f6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "907e5e7f-7e94-57d6-9bfb-14bd7f6ece40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399304} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b34650b-cc77-5f2b-8326-4e505803a14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eab879a0-3375-58dd-903d-5ef4248752b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96231" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcda58d-2a21-5f6a-ba0a-3d3db485ed01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399310} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b0c9cc-603c-54c1-8347-229d13e4b8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399311} {:answer/id #uuid "52e3b75c-95d2-5ae6-832d-7518bf88f670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Y\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399312} {:answer/id #uuid "de318c21-543b-51d3-a702-aafb0894df19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Y\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399313} {:answer/id #uuid "411ec587-a129-5e2e-b6ff-63373524322f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Q\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fcda58d-2a21-5f6a-ba0a-3d3db485ed01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399310} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77443c6-cca3-578d-9d97-40ff1676ac04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "017b78ef-16f0-5056-9e83-f2f23609a4ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-96752" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y.$\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:R\\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $R,$ and the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "390ecc8f-2938-5466-b49b-964a67f79755" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the One-Dimensional Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd28d78-6826-51c4-bb97-c5994d734c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399317} {:answer/id #uuid "0899c6c5-3e81-55cb-a780-9e9332201c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399318} {:answer/id #uuid "10fc3ece-7797-5f53-8829-a11ecea3780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399319} {:answer/id #uuid "9292a981-b609-5970-917e-547c35bcf2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399320} {:answer/id #uuid "a6982ec5-f770-5406-8115-0f127674fbbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9292a981-b609-5970-917e-547c35bcf2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399320} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f8391a-382e-52ac-9e3b-9db6b56f6633" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdcf8d93-f073-5ef6-b0fa-29dd34699bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97071" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(6,2)$ and $(9,3),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-2}{9-6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35cd8ad3-22e0-53ff-b0ef-fe55044a8fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)+2{f}_{z}(c)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399323} {:answer/id #uuid "53c09fde-7f5e-5f4d-945b-3d9da7879a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399324} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc4ace7-5893-5557-936a-3e6b47c57856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399325} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7d2efb-7628-5876-815a-bcb3a7b6b5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+2{f}_{z}(c)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399326} {:answer/id #uuid "6332e767-ea87-5d27-b265-0b9a23c53783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fc4ace7-5893-5557-936a-3e6b47c57856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399325} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3c8411-8b88-57aa-81a6-28759d5876e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a668edb-9a8d-5acd-b6b7-2627f5a72df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98117" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=2$ and $f(b)=3,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 3-2 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4,1⟩-⟨3,2,5⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-4{f}_{z}(c) \\\\ -1 & =2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1.$ *"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77702da-2dc8-56b5-9db1-be0b111ea63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399330} {:answer/id #uuid "cb23530e-d73c-5fed-bfb2-d794a091435b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399331} {:answer/id #uuid "affdcd79-dc85-53c4-b26a-150695001bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399332} {:answer/id #uuid "675e4916-c043-5a86-94e0-b5ce7ed867f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399333} {:answer/id #uuid "66a239a8-93d6-518d-96e1-a3a28f06e1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "affdcd79-dc85-53c4-b26a-150695001bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399332} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca4d33a-4ff2-52b4-8aee-3fbddebc35d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea8f7f21-702e-5433-ab10-60c9a800aee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-98765" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeros outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution\n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a220f89c-3d0a-5463-b736-64bfc352bad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399337} {:answer/id #uuid "293c8357-8265-5b01-b470-7d517f25aa72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399338} {:answer/id #uuid "35a514cb-7982-5a31-a007-45f908842ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399339} {:answer/id #uuid "e92e99cd-068f-5ddb-b8d3-1c72fa9118b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399340} {:answer/id #uuid "63e3470d-66bf-5e47-8c08-2675fd0d4713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a220f89c-3d0a-5463-b736-64bfc352bad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399337} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3279ea5-57df-56e5-a59e-a9baf5354ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4670b2c4-578c-543d-913f-4a2e02949175" :question/math-academy-id "q-98768" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badd1b57-14e7-5b56-b3e2-4f76bf483962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399344} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc1b791-33fe-5cbe-8db2-7cec317b3415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399345} {:answer/id #uuid "9947a22d-6748-5635-9bff-c054c9d5412e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399346} {:answer/id #uuid "a32a5598-4875-50b4-a207-219749ce447a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399347} {:answer/id #uuid "39ea83b4-6e8e-5133-9645-9dd52053a38b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "badd1b57-14e7-5b56-b3e2-4f76bf483962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399344} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d542a7-e1e6-5040-8811-6896030adc94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99338d6e-762d-5e46-924e-c9b8ff055bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99009" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08059ff9-d83f-540a-a482-2a8f806a18c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399351} {:answer/id #uuid "40d83586-f330-5ddd-a884-c230dd198024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399352} {:answer/id #uuid "591f3fa0-6797-5340-8094-49d07c9c3d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399353} {:answer/id #uuid "44d35100-e5d6-5e35-ac5b-f307d1753f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399354} {:answer/id #uuid "1396c469-16f9-566a-aa93-62900910c424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "591f3fa0-6797-5340-8094-49d07c9c3d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399353} :answer-field/id #uuid "25347463-2d58-522d-8979-b8544d1335f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e5f2d5d-b1b9-503b-9dbd-36de49acff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-99204" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $108.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}yz=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}xyz=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
